# Target Statement

Let T be a tree on n vertices, and define

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil.
$$

Let n2(T) be the number of vertices of degree exactly 2.

We want to prove:

$$
\boxed{
n_2(T)\le2q-2
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
b(T)\le q.
}
$$

Equivalently, the desired structural lemma is:

$$
\boxed{
b(T)\ge q+1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
n_2(T)\ge2q-1.
}
$$

The second formulation is the primary target.

# Computational Evidence Already Established

The exact validated burning-number algorithm has exhaustively verified the statement for every non-isomorphic tree with

$$
5\le n\le19.
$$

In particular:

* n <= 16: all non-isomorphic trees individually available;
* n = 17: complete exact filtered search;
* n = 18: complete exact filtered search;
* n = 19: all 288,198 trees satisfying n2 <= 8 were exactly evaluated;
* no genuine counterexample was found.

Thus there is currently no known counterexample in the tested range.

Previously observed high-burning-number obstruction families include subdivided stars.

For the family T_r consisting of:

* r-1 long arms of actual length r-1;
